WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.821
Task statement 1.7.

2
00:00:01.821 --> 00:00:05.019
Manage session state, resumption, and forking.

3
00:00:05.019 --> 00:00:16.628
Resuming yesterday's analysis after teammates changed the code can leave the agent reasoning over stale file reads, so knowing when to resume, fork or restart matters.

4
00:00:18.028 --> 00:00:23.975
A session is the saved conversation, including every file the agent read and every result it got.

5
00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:25.262
You have three moves.

6
00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:28.328
Resume is like opening a bookmark and carrying on.

7
00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:32.865
Forking is like photocopying the notebook so you can try a different path.

8
00:00:32.865 --> 00:00:37.463
And a fresh start is a new notebook with a short summary page at the front.

9
00:00:38.863 --> 00:00:42.195
Give your sessions names so you can come back to them.

10
00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:45.527
Resume by name to continue exactly where you left off.

11
00:00:45.527 --> 00:00:53.239
Add the fork session flag to branch into a new session that starts with the same history, while the original stays untouched.

12
00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:58.916
In the Agent SDK, you pass the session ID to resume, and set fork session to true to branch.

13
00:01:00.316 --> 00:01:01.474
Here is the catch.

14
00:01:01.474 --> 00:01:05.912
Tool results saved in a session do not refresh when the code changes.

15
00:01:05.912 --> 00:01:12.216
If only a few files changed, resume and tell the agent exactly which ones, so it reads them again.

16
00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:17.491
If a lot changed, start a fresh session and give it a summary of earlier findings.

17
00:01:17.491 --> 00:01:21.415
Also remember a fork copies the conversation, not your files.

18
00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:28.499
In the developer productivity scenario, yesterday's named session mapped the billing module.

19
00:01:28.499 --> 00:01:31.094
Overnight, a teammate changed three files.

20
00:01:31.094 --> 00:01:35.667
You resume and say exactly which files changed, so the agent rereads them.

21
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.385
Then you want to compare two refactor ideas.

22
00:01:38.385 --> 00:01:44.873
You fork the session twice, so both branches start from the same analysis and neither pollutes the other.

23
00:01:46.273 --> 00:01:48.106
Exam items here test judgment.

24
00:01:48.106 --> 00:01:52.322
Resuming is great, but only if you tell the agent what changed since.

25
00:01:52.322 --> 00:01:59.410
When the codebase has moved a lot, a fresh session seeded with a summary is safer than dragging along stale results.

26
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:06.497
And when you want to compare options fairly, fork from the shared baseline instead of trying them one after another.

27
00:02:07.897 --> 00:02:08.454
To recap.

28
00:02:08.454 --> 00:02:10.742
Resume a named session to keep going.

29
00:02:10.742 --> 00:02:15.196
Fork when you want to explore alternatives from the same starting point.

30
00:02:15.196 --> 00:02:18.412
When you resume, tell the agent which files changed.

31
00:02:18.412 --> 00:02:25.092
And if the ground has shifted a lot, start fresh with a clear summary rather than trusting old tool results.

